LISPTC LANGUAGE REFERENCE

Lisptc: Common-Lisp-like dialect. One interpreter, cons cells, symbols, lexical
scope, macros, tail-call optimization, small standard library called the
prelude. This reference is authoritative: data types, every built-in, standard
library. Name not listed here does not exist — define it yourself.

SYNTAX

Source is s-expressions. (f a b) calls f with evaluated a and b.

Only parenthesised top-level forms are program text. Text around them is prose,
ignored. Writing "let me square it" around (sq 5) evaluates only the form.
Bare value at top level — 42, "done", my-var — is prose, not an expression:
wrap it in a form. Stray paren in prose still reads as code, so never write
one; paren smiley unsayable.

NEVER PUT A SENTENCE IN PARENTHESES. Every paren reads as code, so a wrapped
remark is a call: (the page loaded, title is "hyko") calls the with five
arguments, fails with undefined: the. Put remark as bare prose beside the form.
No bracket makes a comment, any shape.

Aside whose head names nothing — (see below) , (one, two,
  three) — reads as prose, comes back as skipped note. Mistake the reader
forgave, not a way to write. Forgiveness narrow, unpredictable: anything shaped
like a call runs. Keyword argument, string literal, nested call, or slashed or
underscored name with no words around it — (server/tool :key "value") , dummy
names — is code whatever is missing. (see below) skips; (see
  "below") raises undefined: see. Tool of unloaded server: undefined: error to
fix, not a skip. Reply of only asides ran nothing, ends the loop, see "Ending
the loop".

To see a result, name it in a form: (echo search-mcps-1) . Bare name
search-mcps-1 on its own line is prose, evaluates nothing. Prose-only reply
ENDS THE LOOP with that line as final answer. Most expensive typo in the
language.

- Quote: 'x same as (quote x) — x unevaluated.
- Quasiquote: leading backtick same as (quasiquote x) . Inside, ,x unquotes,
  inserts evaluated x; ,@x unquote-splicing, splices a list.
  Example: (a ,(+ 1 2) ,@(list 3 4)) evaluates to the list a 3 3 4.
- Lists: (a b c) . Dotted pair: (a . b) is one cons cell.
- Strings: "..." with escapes \\ \" \n \r \f \b \t \v. No character type:
  "character" is one-character string.
- Keywords: :foo self-evaluating, interned, so (eq :a :a) is t.
- Numbers: see NUMBERS. nil and () same value: empty list and false. t is
  canonical true.
- Comments: there is no comment syntax — prose around forms is the comment.
  Semicolon is ordinary symbol character, so "; note" inside a form is code.
- Symbols: any token not number, string, keyword, nil or t. / and - legal in
  names, e.g. string-split, acme/get_widget.

DATA TYPES

- nil = () = null = empty list = the ONLY false value.
- t canonical true. Everything except nil truthy, including 0, "", empty
  structures.
- Cons cell — pair (car . cdr) , list is chain of cells ending in nil.
- Symbol — interned name; gensym and make-symbol make uninterned ones,
  printed #:name.
- Keyword — :foo, self-evaluating.
- String — text; no char type.
- Number — exact bigint integer or inexact float.
- Function / closure / macro — from lambda, defun, defmacro.
- Secret — tainted string wrapper, only when secrets extension loaded.

Test emptiness with null or not. Never rely on 0 or "" being false — they are
TRUE.

SPECIAL FORMS

Evaluated specially, not as function calls. Complete list:

| Form | Shape | Meaning |
|------|-------|---------|
| quote | (quote x) | Return x unevaluated, written 'x. |
| progn | (progn e...) | Evaluate in order, return the last. |
| cond | (cond (test body...) ...) | First clause with non-nil test: eval its body, return last, or the test value if no body. Else nil. |
| setq | (setq name val ...) | Assign val to variable name, one or more pairs. Returns last value. Defines or updates a global if name isn't a local. |
| lambda | (lambda (args...) body...) | Anonymous function, a closure over the current scope. |
| macro | (macro (args...) body...) | Anonymous macro. Top-level only; normally use defmacro. |
| quasiquote | (quasiquote x) | Template with unquote and unquote-splicing, see the syntax section. |
| try | (try
  body
  (catch (e)
    handler...)) | Eval body; on error, bind e to the error value and run the handler. |

if, when, unless, and, or,
let, let*, letrec, while, dolist, dotimes, case, defun,
defmacro are prelude macros, not special forms; use the same way. No setf, no
block and return-from, no unwind-protect, no throw and catch pair.

Tail calls optimized: tail recursion and loop macros never overflow the stack.
Iterate with tail-recursive helpers or loop macros.

NUMBERS

- Integer literal is exact bigint: 42.
- Decimal or exponent makes inexact float: 42.0, 4.2e1.
- + - * keep exactness: bigint with bigint gives bigint. ANY float makes the
  result float, "float contagion": (+ 1 2) is 3,
(+ 1 2.0) is 3.0, (* 2 3) is 6, (* 2 3.0) is 6.0.
- / is ALWAYS float, even evenly divisible: (/ 6 2)
is 3.0. Exact integer division: truncate, gives bigint:
(truncate 6 2) is 3.
- Integer-valued float prints 3.0; bigint prints 3.
- = and eql compare numerically across types: (= 3 3.0) is t.
- Divide by zero: / follows JS, (/ 1 0) is Infinity, (/ 0 0) is NaN, no error.
  Integer truncate, % and mod on bigints throw. Guard divisors.

Arithmetic built-ins:

- (+ n...) sum, 0 if none · (* n...) product, 1 if none ·
(- n m...) subtract, or negate one arg · (/ n m...) float divide.
- (% x y) remainder, sign of x, alias rem · (mod x y) modulo, sign of y ·
(truncate x [y]) toward zero, gives bigint.
- (< x y) primitive less-than. Prelude adds (> x y) , (>= x y) , (<= x y) ,
(/= x y) and (= x y) .
- (numberp x) only numeric predicate. There are NO zerop, evenp,
  abs, min, max, floor, ceiling, round, expt, sqrt or gcd — build them:
  even is (= 0 (% n 2)) , abs is (if (< n 0) (- n) n) .

PRIMITIVE BUILT-INS, DEFINED IN THE INTERPRETER CORE

Lists & pairs
- (car x) first · (cdr x) rest · (cons a b) new cell · (list x...) .
- (length x) length of list or string, bigint.
- (rplaca cell x) and (rplacd cell x) destructively set car and cdr, aliases
  setcar and setcdr.

Predicates
- (atom x) true if not cons · (eq x y) identity · (eql x y) identity or
  numeric and string equality, alias = · (stringp x) · (numberp x) .

Symbols
- (gensym) fresh uninterned symbol · (make-symbol name) · (intern name) ·
(symbol-name sym) .

Strings
- (char s i) one-char string at i, or nil · (concat s...) concatenate
  · (string-upcase s) · (string-downcase s) .
- (string x) any value to string, its printed form; string stays itself,
  unquoted: (string 12) is "12", (string 'foo) is
  "foo", (string nil) is "nil". concat takes strings only, so number goes
  through string first:
(concat "Chapter " (string n)) . Float keeps .0: (string 3.0)
is "3.0"; (string (truncate x)) gives bare integer.

Parsing text into data
- (read s) parse FIRST Lisp expression in s, return as data, unevaluated:
  (read "(+ 1 2)") gives the list (+ 1 2) , not 3. Trailing text ignored;
  no expression errors.
- (json-parse s) parse JSON: object to alist with string keys, array to list,
  true to t, false and null to nil. Numbers arrive as floats: 7 reads 7.0; =
  still compares numerically.
  (cdr (assoc "title" (json-parse s))) reads a field.
- Different parsers, not aliases. read on JSON returns junk, no error: { is
  ordinary symbol character, so {"a": 1} reads as symbol {. json-parse for
  JSON, read for Lisp.

Eval / control
- (apply f args) call f with elements of list args.
- (error value) raise catchable error carrying value, caught by try.
- (break) exit nearest loop; (return value) exit it with value.

Output — echo is the ONLY thing that prints. Nothing computed is seen until
echoed.
- (echo x...) print args space-separated, one trailing newline: strings
  as-is, rest re-readable. (echo) is blank line.
  Keyword prints as itself only when nothing follows — (echo :ok) prints :ok —
  since keyword followed by value reads as option. Put keyword to print LAST,
  or wrap in list.

Introspection
- (doc name) print binding's signature and doc; (doc) lists all documented
  names. Read it before guessing an argument, and again after a rejected call.
- (dump) list every global symbol · (import "path") load and eval Lisp file.

Globals: *gensym-counter*, *version*.

PRELUDE STANDARD LIBRARY

All available by default.

List access — composed car and cdr: caar cadr cdar cddr caaar caadr cadar
caddr cdaar cdadr cddar cdddr. cadr is 2nd element, caddr 3rd, and so on.

List building / manipulation
- (append list...) concatenate lists, non-destructive.
- (nreverse list) destructive reverse · (last list) last cons cell ·
(nconc lists...) destructive concat.
- (mapcar f list) new list of f over each element:
(mapcar (lambda (n) (* n n)) '(1 2 3)) gives (1 4 9) .
- (nth n list) element at index n from 0; nil past end.
- (filter f list) elements f says yes to:
(filter (lambda (n) (< 10 n)) '(3 12 7 40)) gives (12 40) .
- (reduce f list [initial]) fold left: f takes accumulator and element.
  No initial: first element starts; empty list gives nil.
  (reduce + '(1 2 3)) is 6. List SECOND, initial last.
- No mapc, remove, elt or sort. Build with filter, mapcar or dolist.

Predicates
- (not x) and (null x) true if nil · (consp x) true if cons ·
(listp x) true if nil or cons · (keywordp x) true if keyword ·
(equal x y) deep structural equality.

Membership / alists
- (memq key list) tail from first eq match · (member key list) first equal
  match · (assq key alist) first pair with eq car · (assoc key alist) first
  pair with equal car. Alist field:
(cdr (assoc "content" msg)) .
- (get-in record key...) read through nested alists, keyword plists and lists,
  every step guarded: (get-in config "server" "port") is nil when no
  "server", not an error. No or guard, no assoc chain.
  Number key indexes a list — (get-in reply "results" 0 "url") — keyword key
  matches string of its name, :port finds "port". Plist key matches by name:
(get-in '(:theme (:accent "red")) :theme :accent) gives "red" .

Nested data, write side of get-in. Pure: return new copy, record unchanged.
- (set-in record value key...) value at path. Missing key added: plist gets
  keyword, alist gets pair; nil record becomes plist for keyword key, else alist.
  Index replaces element, index = length appends:
(set-in '(:theme (:accent "red")) "blue" :theme :accent) gives (:theme (:accent "blue")) .
- (update-in record f key...) value at path becomes (f value); f gets nil when
  path missing:
(update-in '(:n 1) (lambda (n) (+ n 1)) :n) gives (:n 2) .
- (remove-in record key...) drop plist key, alist pair or list index; missing
  path gives record back:
(remove-in '(:a 1 :b 2) :a) gives (:b 2) .
- Edit data file: read it, set-in / update-in / remove-in, write whole value back.

Control-flow macros
- (if test then else...) · (when test body...) · (unless test body...) ·
(and x...) , nil on first nil, else last · (or x...) , first non-nil ·
(case key (keys forms...) ... (t default...)) — matches key by equal
  against each clause's key list; t clause is default.

Binding macros
- (let ((name val) ...) body...) parallel bindings; bare name binds nil.
- (let* ((name val) ...) body...) sequential; each val may use earlier names:
(let* ((issue (car issues)) (state (cdr (assoc "state" issue)))) …) .
- (letrec ((name val) ...) body...) bindings reference each other — local
  recursive functions.

Loops, all support (break) and (return value)
- (while test body...) .
- (dolist (name list [result]) body...) iterate elements.
- (dotimes (name count [result]) body...) iterate 0..count-1.

Defining
- (defun name (args...) [doc] body...) define function; &rest x variadic.
- &key after required args makes the rest optional and nameable:
  (defun kpi (&key label value unit) ...) is called as
(kpi "Revenue" 42000) or (kpi :label "Revenue" :value 42000 :unit "USD") .
  Filled left to right until first keyword; missing one is nil.
  &key and &rest cannot both appear.
- (defmacro name (args...) [doc] body...) define macro.

Strings
- (string= s1 s2) string content equality, same test as equal ·
(substring s start [end]) · (string-prefix? p s) · (string-suffix? p s) ·
(string-index s sub [start]) index or nil · (string-contains? s sub) ·
(string-count s sub) · (string-replace s old new) ·
(string-split s sep) , empty sep gives characters · (string-join list sep) ·
(string-trim s) .
- (format template arg...) fill template, return string: ~a inserts next arg
  as string would, ~% newline, ~~ tilde; letter case-insensitive.
  (format "The sum was ~a" n) writes a sentence around a value. Prints
  nothing: echo result.

Misc
- (identity x) return x.
- (think part...) echo reasoning as narration: parts print literally, ,x part
  evaluated first, e.g. (think "sum is" ,(+ 1 2)) . Returns nil; real answer
  goes in separate expression.

MACROS

defmacro defines compile-time transformer. Build expansion with quasiquote:

(defmacro swap (a b)
  (let ((tmp (gensym)))
    `(let ((,tmp ,a)) (setq ,a ,b) (setq ,b ,tmp))))

(gensym) for temporaries you introduce, so user variables are not captured.
Expansion depth max 20 nesting levels at compile time.

ERRORS & CONTROL FLOW

- (error value) raises; value any Lisp value, often string.
- (try
  expr
  (catch (e)
    handler...)) evaluates expr; on raise, e bound to raised value, handler
  runs. ONLY error mechanism — no condition system, no unwind-protect.

(try
  (error "boom")
  (catch (e)
    (concat "failed: " e)))

- (break) and (return value) escape nearest while, dolist or dotimes. Outside
  a loop: error.

GOTCHAS

- Only nil is false — 0, 0.0, "" all TRUE.
- / gives float; truncate for exact integer.
- Output shows 3 vs 3.0.
- No mapc or math library — write them with reduce, filter, mapcar, dolist or
  recursion.
- read parses LISP, json-parse parses JSON. read on JSON returns junk, no error.
- JSON numbers are floats: integer id prints 7.0; = and eql still equal 7.
- No character type: one-character strings.
- No comment syntax: remarks in prose around forms, never inside one.
- No print, princ, terpri or view — echo does all.
- Hash-and-angle-brackets printout — closure, live value reader cannot read
  back — is printout, not syntax; typing one always errors. Use the name the
  value was reported under.
